# message_route:
# CSES 1667, Message Route (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1667), in our own
# words: computers 1 to computer_count joined by undirected connections, given as a list
# of pairs; the fewest computers on a route from computer 1 to computer computer_count, or
# none when there is no route. The answer is a Scott option: none = λwhen_none when_some. when_none,
# some value = λwhen_none when_some. when_some value. CSES also prints one such route; a shortest route need
# not be unique, so the problem asks for its length alone. Part of the CSES
# port's first batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: walks length computer holds when some walk of
# exactly length connections leads from computer to computer_count, by its recursion on length (length = 0: computer is
# computer_count; otherwise some connection at computer leads to a computer other_computer with walks (length - 1)
# other_computer); the answer is the least length below computer_count with walks length 1, plus one, since a
# shortest walk visits no computer twice and so has fewer than computer_count connections.
# Under tabling the reading shares walks length computer for each length and computer, about computer_count connection_count
# steps for connection_count connections; the replacement the family asks for is
# breadth-first search, about computer_count + connection_count.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ, application and
# top, with no choice and no parallel outside top. It β-reduces to V, the Scott
# option of the canonical numeral of the answer (the search consumes the bound computer_count
# - length, walks consumes length, each fold a finite list): every top it holds (a bit of
# a literal that no key expects) stands in a branch that a Scott boolean
# discards, and binary_subtract, which writes no top (../common/binary_modular),
# is never asked for less than zero, since the program subtracts only what is no
# larger. So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable equations
# for the contextual preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "boolean")
λ true false _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero _ binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment _ _ merge_binary_equality
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "order")
λ binary_less_than _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "cses") "common") "binary_modular")
λ _ binary_subtract _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λwhen_none when_some. when_none)
λ none
. (λtuple. tuple λvalue when_none when_some. when_some value)
λ some
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ computer_count connections
    . fixpoint
    λ self length computer
    . (λany. length (merge_binary_equality computer computer_count) (λ_. any connections) λ_. any connections)
    λ
    . fixpoint
    λ any rest
    . rest false
    λ connection others
    . connection
    λ first_computer second_computer
    . ( λ previous
        . merge_binary_equality first_computer computer (self previous second_computer true λ. any others) λ. merge_binary_equality second_computer computer (self previous first_computer true λ. any others) λ. any others
      ) λ. binary_subtract length (binary_odd binary_zero) false
  )
λ walks
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ computer_count connections
    . fixpoint
      ( λ self length
        . binary_less_than length computer_count (walks computer_count connections length (binary_odd binary_zero) (some λ. merge_binary_increment length) λ. self λ. merge_binary_increment length) none
      ) binary_zero
  )
λ message_route
. export message_route
